---
/**
 * /about — what this is, who it is for, how mature it is, and where the work
 * happens.
 *
 * Every claim here is checkable in this repository: the version policy is
 * `docs/VERSIONING.md` (linked through the docs origin), the maturity limits are
 * `SECURITY.md` and the changelog, the spelling contract is `docs/NAMING.md`,
 * and the corpus counts come from `@data/evidence.json`. There is no founding
 * story and no dates, because neither is sourced anywhere in the tree.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHeader from '@ui/SectionHeader.astro';
import Prose from '@ui/Prose.astro';
import Callout from '@ui/Callout.astro';
import Stat from '@ui/Stat.astro';
import Button from '@ui/Button.astro';

import surfaces from '@data/surfaces.json';
import evidence from '@data/evidence.json';

const docs = 'https://docs.pherolang.com';
const repo = 'https://github.com/yestechgroup/pherolang';
const partnerEmail = 'hello@pherolang.com';
const partnerMail = `mailto:${partnerEmail}?subject=Design%20partner`;
---

<Base
  title="About — Phero Lang"
  description="What Phero Lang is, who it is for, how mature it is (pre-1.0), the naming rule, and where the work happens."
>
  <section class="pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--text-muted)]">About</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-[24ch] font-display text-display-1 font-semibold text-balance">
      What this is, and what it is not yet.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-lg leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text-muted)] text-pretty">
      An open-source modelling language and compiler for systems whose specifications are too
      important to live in four tools that never meet. It is pre-1.0, and this page says exactly what
      that means.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="01 / What this is"
      title="A compiler, a wire format, and every surface"
      lede="Not a diagram tool with an export button. The compiler checks a model, lowers it to a versioned artifact, and that artifact is what everything downstream reads."
    />

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr]">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Phero Lang gives each kind of specification its own small language — a domain model, an
          application design, an authorisation policy, a decision model, an event contract, a use case
          pattern, a case model, an interaction flow, a deployment projection. They share one type
          index, one expression language and one diagnostics registry, so a check in one can see a
          declaration in another.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each surface compiles to a JSON artifact with a version gate. Rust code generators, JSON
          Schema, Cedar policy, a DMN engine model and a CMMN case definition all read those
          artifacts and nothing above them. That boundary is the design: it is why a backend can be
          written outside this repository, and why a grammar change cannot quietly alter generated
          code.
        </p>
        <p>
          The whole implementation is a Rust workspace, released as one binary,
          <code>pherolang</code>. The documentation in this repository is the same documentation the
          docs site renders — there is no second copy to drift.
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="h-fit rounded-md border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--surface-raised)] p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          The example corpus
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <Stat value={String(surfaces.surfaces.length)} label="Surfaces" />
          <Stat value={String(evidence.counts.crates)} label="Crates" />
          <Stat value={String(evidence.counts.applications)} label="Applications" />
          <Stat value={String(evidence.counts.nodes)} label="Graph nodes" />
        </div>
        <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          Compiled by <code>{evidence.generator.reported}</code> from
          <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples`} rel="noopener">examples/</a>, which CI compiles,
          generates and executes on every change.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="who" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="02 / Who it is for"
      title="Three kinds of team, and one kind that should wait"
      lede="A modelling language is a long commitment. It is fairer to say who will get value from this one now, and who will not."
    />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="border-t-2 border-[var(--border-strong)] pt-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">
          Teams whose domain is the hard part
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          Lending, insurance, health, logistics, internal platforms: anywhere the rules are the
          product, authorisation is part of correctness, and a decision table outliving its author is
          a liability. These are the systems the surfaces were shaped against.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t-2 border-[var(--border-strong)] pt-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">
          Platform engineers who generate code
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          If you already write generators against a schema and you are tired of the schema being
          whatever the last parser produced, the artifact contract is the interesting part. A backend
          is a function; it needs no compiler changes and no grammar knowledge.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t-2 border-[var(--border-strong)] pt-5">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">
          People who need the model to outlive the tool
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          Artifacts are committed, byte-compared and validated without the compiler. A model is a file
          in your repository, not a row in somebody's database — which is also why it can be reviewed
          like code.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 max-w-[72ch]">
      <Callout tone="warning" title="And who should wait">
        <p>
          If you need a visual modeller, a hosted registry, a plugin marketplace, or a wire format
          that will not change this year, this is not it yet. If your system is a thin CRUD app with
          no decisions, no authorisation model and no lifecycle, the modelling layers will cost more
          than they return.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="maturity" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="03 / Maturity"
      title="Pre-1.0, with the rules written down"
      lede="Version numbers are a promise. This is the promise, in the terms the project actually publishes."
    />

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr]">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          <strong>0.x does not promise stability.</strong> Grammar, artifact shape and CLI surface may
          change at a minor version. What is promised is disclosure: every break lands in the
          changelog with the golden-regeneration notes needed to see exactly what drifted.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>1.0 freezes the wire formats.</strong> From then on, within a
          <code>formatVersion</code>, evolution is additive only: new optional fields, no renames, no
          changed meanings, no artifact that stops parsing. A <code>formatVersion</code> bump is the
          only sanctioned break, and it has to be a major release with a migration note.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One exception has already been spent.</strong> The 0.3 release does not read 0.2
          artifacts. When the product was renamed for 0.3, the provenance key, the instance
          discriminator and the schema identifier all moved with it, so every artifact that depended
          on them moved to <code>formatVersion 2</code>. That was the single allowed pre-1.0 wire
          break; a saved older artifact fails <code>artifact check</code> with one named migration
          message instead of a generic mismatch.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Support is bounded.</strong> Only the latest 0.x minor receives fixes, and there is
          no commercial support contract, SLA or hosted service. Security reports have a private
          channel and a stated response aim, not a guarantee:
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`} rel="noopener">SECURITY.md</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full policy — what each artifact promises, what a release consists of — is
          <a href={`${docs}/reference/versioning/`}>Versioning and release policy</a>.
        </p>
      </Prose>

      <div class="h-fit rounded-md border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--surface-raised)] p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          Where the guarantees live
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text)]">
          <li>
            <a href={`${docs}/reference/versioning/`}>Versioning</a> — 0.x allowances, the 1.0 freeze,
            the one pre-1.0 wire break.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`${docs}/reference/backends/`}>Backend contract</a> — what an artifact guarantees
            to whoever consumes it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`${docs}/engineering/verification/`}>Proof-obligation register</a> — each claim's
            status and evidence pointer, with a review date on the rows that carry one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`${docs}/engineering/semantics/`}>The claim ladder</a> — including everything the
            project explicitly refuses to claim.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`} rel="noopener">CHANGELOG.md</a> — the drift
            record itself.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="name" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="04 / The name"
      title="Phero Lang, two words"
      lede="A spelling rule, stated once and held mechanically by the repository's own gate."
    />
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-[72ch]">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          The product is <strong>Phero Lang</strong> — two words, never the bare token. The wordmark
          and the command-line binary are <code>pherolang</code>, one word. The library crates are
          <code>phero-*</code>; the repository is <code>yestechgroup/pherolang</code>; the origins are
          <code>pherolang.com</code> and <code>docs.pherolang.com</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is not a style preference. The single token is already the name of unrelated projects,
          so a search for it lands somewhere else; the compound is what this project can own. The rule
          is enforced by a script in the continuous-integration gate, which fails on any revived
          spelling outside a small, documented allowlist — the naming contract is
          <a href={`${docs}/reference/naming/`}>docs/NAMING.md</a>.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="05 / Where the work happens"
      title="In one repository, in the open"
      lede="The compiler, the grammar, the documentation, the examples and this site are in the same Apache-2.0 tree. There is no private core."
    />
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr]">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Bug reports, failing test cases, documentation fixes and new backends are all welcome —
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`} rel="noopener">CONTRIBUTING.md</a> is the path
          from a clone to a merged pull request. The gate is one script;
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml`} rel="noopener">CI runs the same one</a
          >, so a green local run and a green CI run are the same thing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The examples are not samples: <code>examples/</code> is compiled, generated and executed by
          the test suite on every change. If a claim in the documentation stops being true of the
          code, the build fails before the documentation can drift.
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <Button href={repo} variant="secondary">The repository</Button>
        <Button href={`${repo}/issues`} variant="secondary">Issues</Button>
        <Button href={`${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`} variant="ghost">Contributing</Button>
        <Button href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`} variant="ghost">Apache-2.0 licence</Button>
        <Button href={`${repo}/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`} variant="ghost">Code of conduct</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="engage" class="border-t border-[var(--border)] py-14">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="06 / How to engage"
      title="File an issue, or model something with us"
      lede="The fastest useful contribution is a failing case. The most useful conversation is a real domain the current surfaces do not describe well."
    />
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr]">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          For a bug: a minimal reproducible model beats a description. Attach the smallest
          <code>.mox</code> or design file that shows the problem, the output of
          <code>pherolang --version</code>, and what you expected instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          Design partnerships are how the next round of work gets chosen: a few teams at a time,
          working with the maintainers on a real system. It is pre-1.0 tooling with no support
          contract, so the terms are modest in both directions — what a partner gets and what we ask
          is spelled out on <a href="/#design-partners">the homepage</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Suspected vulnerabilities go through a
          <a href={`${repo}/security/advisories/new`} rel="noopener">private advisory</a>, never a
          public issue.
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="h-fit rounded-md border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--surface-raised)] p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          Design partners
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--text)]">
          Write to <a href={partnerMail}>{partnerEmail}</a> with a paragraph about what you are
          modelling and what makes it hard. There is no form and no sales call.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5">
          <Button href={partnerMail}>Write to us</Button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <Button href="/platform/overview" variant="secondary">Platform overview</Button>
      <Button href="/architecture" variant="secondary">Architecture</Button>
      <Button href="/getting-started" variant="ghost">Get started</Button>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
